digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate a closed-loop protein-engineering strategy, PLMeAE, that combines protein-language-model-guided mutant design with automated biofoundry Build/Test workflows to make protein-variant discovery more efficient.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Limitations in conventional and ML-guided protein engineering motivate integrating computational mutant prioritization with automated experimental execution."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Time-consuming experimental collection of functional data is a practical challenge for applying machine learning to protein engineering."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Practical ML-guided protein engineering needs better design priors and efficient experimental workflows because functional-data collection and informative mutant sampling are difficult."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Protein language models are plausible computational tools for modeling protein structure-function relationships."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) Protein language models are plausible prior-knowledge sources for guiding protein mutant selection when structure-function information is limited."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) Biofoundry-supported laboratory automation is valuable for generating the protein-function data needed in ML-guided protein engineering."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) Biofoundries provide relevant laboratory-automation capacity for protein-engineering data-generation workflows."];
  "R26" [label="(0,0,0) The SAMPLE platform is an example of a self-driving autonomous machine developed for fully autonomous protein engineering in a field that has not yet fully leveraged biofoundry advances."];
  "R28" [label="(0,0,0) Protein engineering is broadly relevant because engineered proteins can serve medicine and industrial applications requiring improved properties."];
  "R31" [label="(0,0,0) PLM insights can be used in the Design/Learning stages of a DBTL workflow to infer sequence-fitness relationships and sample mutants."];
  "R39" [label="(0,0,0) Because proteins are important across many fields and industrial proteins often require property improvements, there is a practical need for effective protein-engineering strategies."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Structure- or sequence-guided targeted mutagenesis provides a way to create focused small-but-smart libraries for engineering proteins whose industrial properties need improvement."];
  "n1" [label="(1,0,0) Proteins play a vital role in various fields, including medicine, chemical manufacturing, energy, agriculture, and consumer products."];
  "n11" [label="(7,0,0) Targeted mutagenesis guided by structure or sequence information has become a popular way to produce small-but-smart libraries."];
  "n14" [label="(10,0,0) Choosing a target site for targeted mutagenesis requires a deep understanding of the protein’s structure-function relationship."];
  "n15" [label="(11,0,0) De novo enzyme design remains in its infancy, and tools such as Rosetta and HotSpot Wizard are generally effective only for relatively simple reactions."];
  "n16" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given targeted/site-guided strategies require deep structure–function understanding and existing tools are limited, therefore many proteins lack reliable guidance for choosing mutation sites/substitutions."];
  "n17" [label="(12,0,0) Machine learning has emerged as a promising tool for exploring protein fitness landscapes."];
  "n2" [label="(2,0,0) For industrial applications, proteins often require engineering to enhance properties such as stability, activity, selectivity, and binding affinity."];
  "n25" [label="(20,0,0) Experimentally collecting functional data is time-consuming and labor-intensive, particularly for enzymes with polyspecificity due to substrate diversity and complexity."];
  "n26" [label="(21,0,0) It is difficult to efficiently sample and utilize informative protein mutants to train ML models, especially for proteins without prior structure–function information."];
  "n28" [label="(22,0,0) Protein language models (PLMs) trained on extensive protein sequence datasets learn fundamental principles of protein structure and function."];
  "n3" [label="(2,1,6) The engineering of antibodies has resulted in the production of protein therapeutics with unique properties."];
  "n35" [label="(28,0,0) Laboratory automation aided by biofoundries would be invaluable for generating the large volumes of data needed to develop ML models for protein engineering."];
  "n41" [label="(34,0,0) Protein engineering has yet to fully leverage recent developments in biofoundries."];
  "n42" [label="(35,0,0) A self-driving autonomous machine for protein landscape exploration (SAMPLE) platform was developed for fully autonomous protein engineering."];
  "n48" [label="(40,0,0) In the DBTL cycle, Learning/Design use PLM insights to infer sequence–fitness and sample mutants, while Build/Test are efficiently conducted using automated biofoundries."];
